# WinAppMigrator

> WinAppMigrator is a free, open-source Windows tool that turns an installed desktop app into one self-extracting installer you can run on another PC, even when the original installer is long gone. It reads the app from this PC, from a drive pulled out of a dead machine, from a .vhd or .vhdx image of one, or from an old Windows 7 or later PC over the network through the signed WinAppMigrator Client.

Key facts:

- Platform: Windows 10 and 11, 64-bit; it runs elevated. WinAppMigrator Client runs on Windows 7 and later.
- Price and license: free, MIT license, source at https://github.com/${repository}.
- Current version: ${version}, released ${release_date}. Download: https://github.com/${repository}/releases/latest
  (`WinAppMigrator-v${version}.exe`, a single portable program, Authenticode-signed, with SHA-256 checksums and
  GitHub build-provenance attestations).
- What it captures: the app's files, registry entries, services, drivers, scheduled tasks, shortcuts, file types,
  firewall rules and the runtimes it needs; the user's data and license or activation data only when chosen.
- What the installer does: checks every file's hash before and after it writes, lists everything it will change
  first, can preview without changing anything, and every install can be undone from Settings or by its own
  uninstaller.
- Accessibility: usable with a keyboard alone, a screen reader, Windows' contrast themes and larger text; a
  self-assessed WCAG 2.2 AA conformance report is at https://github.com/${repository}/blob/main/docs/ACCESSIBILITY.md
- Website: ${base_url}/ (this guide), with the landing page and key pages in 24 languages.
